<BlogPost slug="where-your-calendar-password-goes">

  <p>
    Connecting a calendar to an app means handing it a password or a Google sign-in. That's the moment a lot of people stop, and they're right to. Most calendar apps send that credential to their own servers, which then fetch your calendar for you. You're trusting the company with the keys to your schedule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pikos has no server to send it to. Here's where your calendar credential actually goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Into your computer's keychain</h2>

  <p>
    When you connect an iCloud or CalDAV calendar, you type an app-specific password. When you connect Google, you sign in through your browser and Google hands Pikos a token. Either way, Pikos puts it straight into your operating system's keychain: Keychain on a Mac, GNOME Keyring or KWallet on Linux. The keychain encrypts it and only hands it back to Pikos.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Pikos database never holds it. The database knows your account's email or username, so the settings screen can show which account is which, and nothing more. That means a backup, an export, or a copy of your workspace file doesn't carry your password with it. Pikos doesn't write credentials to its logs either.
  </p>

  <h2>Straight to your provider</h2>

  <p>
    When Pikos checks your calendar, your computer talks to Google or your CalDAV server directly. There's no Pikos server in between, because there's no Pikos server at all. Your password only ever leaves your machine to reach the provider it belongs to.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you type a server address that isn't encrypted, Pikos warns you before connecting, since your password would cross the network in the clear. A server on your own machine is the exception.
  </p>

  <h2>Read-only, and only your calendars</h2>

  <p>
    For Google, Pikos asks for two things: the list of your calendars, and read access to their events. It can't create, change, or delete an event, and it can't see your email, contacts, or files. Google's consent screen shows you exactly that before you approve it.
  </p>

  <p>
    For CalDAV, I'd recommend an app-specific password rather than your main one. iCloud requires it anyway. It means you can cut Pikos off from your provider's settings without changing the password you use everywhere else.
  </p>

  <h2>Taking it back</h2>

  <p>
    Disconnecting an account deletes its credential from your keychain. For Google, Pikos also asks Google to revoke the grant, so the token is dead on Google's side too. If that request can't get through, Pikos has still deleted its own copy, and you can remove the grant from your Google account's permissions page.
  </p>

  <p>
    Delete All Data, in Settings → Data, goes further. It removes the stored credential for every calendar account, along with everything else Pikos keeps on your computer.
  </p>

  <h2>Check it yourself</h2>

  <p>
    All of this is in <a href="https://github.com/Pikos-App/pikos">the Pikos code</a>, which is public. If you're curious, the calendar sync code is a short read, and the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> says the same thing in fewer words. When you're ready, the <a href="/calendar-sync">setup guide</a> takes about two minutes.
  </p>

</BlogPost>
